Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/819SkOu-8MA/m/Jpw04nXvDwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear Christian,

there are numerical problems in our analysis, which you can diagnose by the SEs for the abundance covariates that become enormous. As a consequence, the z values become very small and p goes to one.

I am not sure whether the compositional nature of your abundance covariates may be responsible for this; you would have to check what it says about this in the regression literature. Do the SE's blow up already when you add in the first of the abundance covariates or the second, or only once you add in all three ?

Best regards  -- Marc

